userenv: implement DeriveAppContainerSidFromAppContainerName

Qt WebEngine (Qt 6.5.11 / Chromium 122) statically imports
userenv.dll!DeriveAppContainerSidFromAppContainerName from both Qt6WebEngineCore.dll and
QtWebEngineProcess.exe: Chromium's AppContainerBase::Open() calls it whenever the renderer/GPU
AppContainer sandbox is enabled (sandbox/win/src/app_container_base.cc).  Wine has no such export, so
the loader binds the import to its abort thunk (ntdll/loader.c allocate_stub()) and the first call
kills the process with "unimplemented function
userenv.dll.DeriveAppContainerSidFromAppContainerName, aborting"; the neighbouring
CreateAppContainerProfile() is only a FIXME stub returning E_NOTIMPL.

Windows derives the package SID "S-1-15-2-<x0>-<x1>-...-<x6>" from the name: the name is lowercased,
hashed as UTF-16 with SHA-256, and the first 28 bytes of the hash become seven little-endian 32-bit
subauthorities (the eighth 32-bit word is dropped).  The mapping was pinned against the SID Windows 11
itself derives for Microsoft.Windows.ShellExperienceHost_cw5n1h2txyewy,
S-1-15-2-155514346-2573954481-755741238-1654018636-1233331829-3075935687-2861478708, which is exactly
what this implementation returns, and it matches the independent documented reimplementation in
forderud/RunInSandbox (AppContainerSid/Main.cpp: lowercase name, SHA-256 of the UTF-16 bytes, first
28 bytes).  Wine's existing RtlDeriveCapabilitySidsFromName() (dlls/ntdll/sec.c) uses the same scheme
for the capability SID, except that it uppercases the name.

The returned SID lives on the process heap, so the FreeSid() the caller uses (Chromium's
FreeSidDeleter) releases it.  The test in dlls/userenv/tests/userenv.c checks the subauthorities of
that measured SID, that case does not matter (the name is lowercased before hashing), and the two
E_INVALIDARG cases.
